cal states that exist objectively, quantum states of an individual system cannot be found out by an initially ignorant observer through direct measurement without being disrupted. So, to complete the 'quantum theory of the classical' one must identify <i>(iii) quantum origin of objective existence</i> and explain how the information about objectively existing states can appear to be essentially inconsequential for them (as it does for states in Newtonian physics) and yet matter in other settings (e.g., thermodynamics). I show how the mathematical structure of quantum theory supplemented by the only uncontroversial measurement postulate (that demands immediate repeatability-hence, predictability) leads to preferred states. These <i>(i) pointer states</i> correspond to measurement ou

